  "issue": {
    "body": "According to the docs, `Secret` is meant to hide a value from tracebacks, logging, and format statements.\r\n\r\nHowever, since the value is revealed by the `__str__` method, it will still appear in `print`, `logging`, and `format` statements.\r\n\r\n```python\r\nIn [1]: from starlette.datastructures import Secret\r\n\r\nIn [2]: s = Secret('foo')\r\n\r\nIn [3]: print(s)\r\nfoo\r\n\r\nIn [4]: import logging\r\n\r\nIn [5]: logging.critical(s)\r\nCRITICAL:root:foo\r\n\r\nIn [6]: format(s)\r\nOut[6]: 'foo'\r\n```\r\n\r\nThis happening on the latest `master` commit (b94f02e as of this writing).\r\n\r\nOne possible solution would be to hide the value in `__str__` and expose the value by accessing the `value` attribute.\r\n\r\n```python\r\nclass Secret:\r\n    \"\"\"\r\n    Holds a string value that should not be revealed in tracebacks etc.\r\n    You should access the ``value`` attribute at the point it is required.\r\n    \"\"\"\r\n\r\n    def __init__(self, value: str):\r\n        self.value = value\r\n\r\n    def __repr__(self) -> str:\r\n        return \"%s('**********')\" % self.__class__.__name__\r\n\r\n    __str__ = __repr__\r\n```",
    "closed_at": "2019-01-03T16:17:18Z",
    "created_at": "2019-01-03T14:52:44Z",
    "html_url": "https://github.com/Kludex/starlette/issues/301",
